$info=dowild,
$bio

Mr. Do's Wild Ride (c) 01/1984 Universal.


The usual Mr.Do! cherries are present and correct, but are not used in the same way as in other games in the series: collecting them merely changes the 'prize' awarded upon reaching the level's goal. This can be one or two letters from EXTRA, or other items which just score points. When Mr.Do reaches his Goal, he waves a flag while the bonus counter is added to his score. Mr. Do does not have his power ball or hammer in this game, but holding the button makes him run faster along the track, the trade-off being that his bonus points deplete much quicker. When all EXTRA letters are collected, the player is awarded with an extra life. Wild Ride does not seem to feature the intermission screens that appear in previous Mr.Do! games. The first level is a circus roller coaster. The other screens retain the roller coaster track but can be identified by the different obstacles on-screen. The levels are :

* Level 2, pirate ship. 

* Level 3, three elevators. 

* Level 4, castle on screen. 

* Level 5, multiple columns. 

* Level 6, six rings. 

After level six the levels are repeated.


- TECHNICAL -


Main CPU : Z80 (@ 3.9 Mhz), (2x) Z80 (@4mhz)

Sound Chips : (4x) SN76496 (@ 4 Mhz)


Screen orientation : Horizontal

Video resolution : 240 x 192 pixels

Aspect Ratio : 4/3

Screen refresh : 60.00 Hz

Colors palette : 258


Players : 2

Control : 4-way joystick

Buttons : 2


- TRIVIA -


Ocean Software, who were based in the city of Manchester, England, released a home computer version of the game. They obviously decided, however, that the "Mr.Do!" name was no longer commercially strong enough to guarantee strong sales, and instead re-tooled the game as 'Kong Strikes Back' - dropping Nintendo's ubiquitous giant ape into the game and arming the player with bombs - which act in the same way as Mr.Do's powerball.


