$info=starblad,
$bio

Starblade (c) 1991 Namco.


A 3D space shoot'em up featuring two seperate missions; the first is to destroy the planet 'Red Eye' while the second is to wipe out the enemy fleet and their leader. The game's sit-down cabinet featured a seat that vibrated whenever the player was hit, either by incoming fire or by enemy objects such as ships, asteroids etc. The yoke joystick - which had a firebutton situated at the top of the stick, underneath where the player's thumb sits - was very similar in appearence and feel to that used in Atari's 1983 wireframe classic, "Star Wars".


- TECHNICAL -


Namco System 21 hardware

Game ID: ST


Main CPU : (2x) 68000 (@ 12.288 Mhz), M6809 (@ 3.072 Mhz), HD63705 (@ 2.048 Mhz), (2x) TMS32025 (@ 24 Mhz)

Sound Chips : C140 (@ 24 Mhz), YM2151 (@ 3.57958 Mhz)


Screen orientation : Horizontal

Video resolution : 496 x 480 pixels

Aspect Ratio : 4/3

Screen refresh : 60.00 Hz

Colors palette : 32768


Player : 1

Control : stick

Buttons : 4


- TRIVIA -


Starblade was a very obvious influence on both the visual appearence and gameplay of the superb Nintendo Super NES game, ' Starfox'- written in the UK by Argonaut Software.


Victor Entertainment released a limited-edition soundtrack album for this game (Starblade & Galaxian3 - Project Dragon : Namco Game Sound Express Vol.06 - VICL-15009) on 21/03/1992.


